Governs square tin containers of nominal capacity 15 kg or 15 litres. Dimensions must satisfy strict tolerances to ensure proper stacking and volumetric accuracy:
a) Length: 235 ± 3 mm;
b) Width: 235 ± 3 mm;
c) Height: 325 ± 5 mm (conforms to Fig. 1 of the standard).

Tins must be fabricated using high-quality prime electrolytic tinplate of specified grades and thicknesses to resist chemical interaction and maintain physical strength:
a) Single cold-reduced electrolytic tinplate: Nominal thickness of 0.28 mm conforming to IS 1993;
b) Double cold-reduced electrolytic tinplate: Nominal thickness of 0.21 mm conforming to IS 13954.

Requires the material of gauges to be of suitable quality gauge steel capable of retaining dimensions during ageing and heat treatment while remaining wear resistant [3]. Standard steel designations recommended from IS 3748 : 1990 include: 
a) XT 215 Cr 12 for plain plug gauges; 
b) T 105 Cr 5 Mn 2 for ring gauges; 
b) T 105 Cr 5 (typographically printed as T1OSCr5) for thread plug gauges; 
c) T 90 V 2 (typographically printed as T9OV2) for thread ring gauges [3, 4].

Gauges must be legibly marked or tagged (using an aluminium plate for miniature plug gauges) indicating: 
a) Nominal size (per IS 14365); 
b) Class of tolerance with type of deviation and amount of tolerance (plain gauges) or tolerance class (thread gauges only - check plug gauges append 'Tcp' after tolerance class); 
c) The word 'GO' and 'NOT GO' on respective gauges; 
d) Manufacturer's name or trade mark; 
e) Red paint on 'NOT GO' gauges; 
f) 'Check' or 'Wear Check' marked on check plug and wear check plug gauges respectively [4].

Successive graduation marks at 1 m/s and 5 m/s intervals on each scale must have the following precise distances based on the scale factors: 
a) Scale Factor 1 cm = 1 km (2 min time interval): 1.2 mm distance for 1 m/s and 6.0 mm distance for 5 m/s; 
b) Scale Factor 2 cm = 1 km (printed as 12cm=lkm / 2 min time interval): 2.4 mm distance for 1 m/s and 12.0 mm distance for 5 m/s; 
c) Scale Factor 4 cm = 1 km (2 min time interval): 4.8 mm distance for 1 m/s and 24.0 mm distance for 5 m/s.

Prescribes the exact vertical lengths of the engraved graduation lines to ensure clear readability: 
a) 1 m/s marks: 5 mm length; 
b) 5 m/s marks: 8 mm length; 
c) 10 m/s marks: 10 mm length.

Establishes a precise dimensions schedule for twist drill sizes up to 20.00 mm. Sizing parameters dictate exact overall length (l1) and cutting flute length (l2) pairings for each nominal diameter range (d1), including: 
a) Diameter 6.70 to 7.50 mm (l1 = 74 mm, l2 = 34 mm); 
b) Diameter 8.50 to 9.50 mm (l1 = 84 mm, l2 = 40 mm); 
c) Diameter 14.00 to 15.00 mm (l1 = 111 mm, l2 = 56 mm); 
d) Diameter 19.00 to 20.00 mm (l1 = 131 mm, l2 = 66 mm).

Specifies standard surface finish values across the different functional zones of the twist drill as illustrated in the technical drawings: 
a) N6 roughness grade for the parallel shank diameter (d2) and cutting diameter (d1); 
b) N7 roughness grade for the point angle cutting lips; 
c) N9 roughness grade (with alternative N7 or N6 finishes) inside the helical flutes.

Establishes a precise dimensions schedule for solid carbide end mills up to 20.00 mm. For each cutting diameter range (d1), continuous parallel shanks require identical shank diameters (d2) paired with short or long flute lengths (L2), overall length (L1), and gripping length (L3): 
a) Diameter 16.0 mm: Short series flute L2 = 26 mm / Long series flute L2 = 32 mm, L1 = 92 mm, L3 = 48 mm; 
b) Diameter 18.0 mm: Short series flute L2 = 26 mm / Long series flute L2 = 32 mm, L1 = 92 mm, L3 = 48 mm; 
c) Diameter 20.0 mm: Short series flute L2 = 32 mm / Long series flute L2 = 38 mm, L1 = 104 mm, L3 = 50 mm.

Imposes strict upper limits on flute lengths (L2) to prevent excessive overhang: 
a) Up to and including 10 mm: +1.0 / 0 mm; 
b) Over 10 mm up to and including 22 mm: +1.5 / 0 mm; 
c) Over 22 mm: +2.0 / 0 mm.

Specifies standard surface finish values across the different functional zones of the end mill as illustrated in the technical drawings: 
a) N6 roughness grade for the active outer cutting teeth/flutes (d1) and the parallel shank (d2); 
b) N9 roughness grade (with alternative N6 finish) inside the neck or clearance recess region between the helical flutes and the shank.

Each nut size must withstand specified proof load testing: 
a) Class 8S: M16 (168.9 kN), M20 (263.4 kN), M22 (325.7 kN), M24 (379.5 kN), M27 (403.4 kN), M30 (603.1 kN), M36 (878.3 kN); 
b) Class 10S: M16 (195.5 kN), M20 (305.0 kN), M22 (377.0 kN), M24 (439.0 kN), M27 (467.0 kN), M30 (698.0 kN), M36 (1016.0 kN).
